From: Sahan Nissanka <sanoptional@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 11:05:00 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] libcamera: sensor: Add measured sensor delays for OV5675

The ov5675 entry has carried an empty sensorDelays since it was added, so
libcamera falls back to its defaults and logs:

  WARN CameraSensor camera_sensor_legacy.cpp:502 'ov5675 1-0036':
  No sensor delays found in static properties. Assuming unverified defaults.

Measure them instead of guessing.

Method: stream raw Bayer straight off the sensor via V4L2, bypassing
libcamera entirely - libcamera applies controls using the very delay model
being measured, so measuring through it would be circular. With the pipeline
configured by hand (sensor -> CSI-2 -> capture node), step one control
immediately after dequeuing a frame and find the first subsequent frame that
changes. Exposure and gain are observed as a step in mean brightness, VBLANK
as a step in the interval between buffer timestamps.

Nine runs per control on an OV5675 (Dell Latitude 7320 Detachable, IPU6,
1296x972 SGRBG10, 8 buffers). Runs where the buffer sequence numbers were not
consecutive were discarded, since a dropped frame invalidates the counting.

  exposure       8/9 runs agree,  32.4 -> 36.4 mean
  analogue_gain  9/9 runs agree,  36.4 -> 72.7 mean
  vblank         9/9 runs agree,  33.4 -> 85.1 ms frame interval

In every case the change first appeared two frames after the one in flight
when the control was written: the write is issued microseconds after
dequeuing frame S, frame S+1 was already latched during S's readout, and the
change lands in S+2. That is a delay of 2 by the definition used here.

The VBLANK result also checks out arithmetically, which is a useful
cross-check on the whole measurement chain. With HTS = 1296 + 1704 = 3000 and
pixel_rate = 180 MHz the line time is 16.67 us, so 972 + 1048 = 2020 lines is
33.7 ms and 972 + 4192 = 5164 lines is 86.1 ms, against 33.4 ms and 85.1 ms
measured - within about 1%%.

hblankDelay was not measured: V4L2_CID_HBLANK is read-only on this sensor
(min = max = 1704), so it can never be written and the delay is unobservable.
It is set to 2 for consistency with the rest of this file rather than left at
zero; since the control cannot change, the value is never used.

These values match every other OmniVision sensor in this file that has
measured delays (ov5647, ov7251, ov9281, ov08d10, ov64a40 are all 2/2/2/2),
which is reassuring but was not assumed - the measurement was taken first.

Signed-off-by: Sahan Nissanka <sanoptional@gmail.com>
---
 src/libcamera/sensor/camera_sensor_properties.cpp | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/libcamera/sensor/camera_sensor_properties.cpp b/src/libcamera/sensor/camera_sensor_properties.cpp
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/src/libcamera/sensor/camera_sensor_properties.cpp
+++ b/src/libcamera/sensor/camera_sensor_properties.cpp
@@ -383,8 +383,13 @@ const CameraSensorProperties *CameraSensorProperties::get(const std::string &sen
 		{ "ov5675", {
 			.unitCellSize = { 1120, 1120 },
 			.testPatternModes = {
 				{ controls::draft::TestPatternModeOff, 0 },
 				{ controls::draft::TestPatternModeColorBars, 1 },
 			},
-			.sensorDelays = { },
+			.sensorDelays = {
+				.exposureDelay = 2,
+				.gainDelay = 2,
+				.vblankDelay = 2,
+				.hblankDelay = 2,
+			},
 		} },
